Chapter 2. Literature Review and Source-Checked Precedents

2.1 Architectural precedents

2.1.1 The GRID / UN/CEFACT pilot

Status: $\mathcal{V}$ (Validated). The GRID Pilot implements a Sovereign Node architecture: states retain data in their own repositories; a central directory harvests only data cryptographically signed by the registered sovereign identity key. The source states: "The GRID Pilot is a decentralized system where 'Sovereign Nodes' (pilot representatives of Authoritative Registrars of UN Member States) manage their own data in their own repositories." The project documents confirm the principle of "Agnostic Sovereignty: Explicit protection for the 'Sovereign Choice' of UN Member States regarding their internal identifier standards."

Scope of claim. This is a source-checked architectural precedent for sovereignty preservation in a real registry pilot. It authenticates registrars; it does not verify behavior.

2.1.2 Perry & Zhukov (2025), "Cryptographic Data Exchange for Nuclear Warheads"

Status: $\mathcal{V}$ (Validated). The paper (arXiv 2507.20074, July 2025) proposes a cryptographic protocol using chained Merkle commitments, zkSNARK proofs, and selective-opening challenges for warhead tracking without intrusive physical inspection. The paper addresses "nuclear arms control treaties [that] have historically focused on strategic nuclear delivery systems, leaving nuclear warheads outside formal verification frameworks."

Scope of claim. A zkSNARK proves consistency of committed data, not that the data is true. The physical-to-digital boundary is the real weak link.

2.1.3 The UN Pact for the Future (2024) and Global Digital Compact

These instruments recognize the need for verifiable cooperation but do not include an independent, binding verification mechanism with legal force.

2.1.4 Byzantine fault tolerance

Status: $\mathcal{V}$ (Validated). The classical result establishes that the Byzantine Generals Problem is solvable if and only if more than two-thirds of the actors are loyal, for unsigned messages. The threshold is confirmed: "We assume a system of $n = 3f + 1$ processes, out of which at most $f$ can be Byzantine." BFT secures agreement on the ledger, not the truth of its inputs.

2.2 Historical verification regimes

2.2.1 IAEA safeguards

Status: $\mathcal{V}$ (Validated). The IAEA safeguards system demonstrates that verification effectiveness is downstream of political will and power asymmetries, not upstream. The criticism that safeguards are discriminatory is confirmed: Iran's nuclear chief accused the IAEA of "discriminatory approaches to nuclear issues," and a source refers to the "Nuclear apartheid of the IAEA." These are politicized sources, but the structural critique is corroborated by the broader academic literature on the NPT regime.

2.2.2 OPCW and legitimacy contestation

Status: $\mathcal{V}$ (Validated). The extended attribution role of the OPCW was "controversial and remains on legally contested ground." Russia has explicitly "rejected the legitimacy of the IIT's mandate," calling it an "illegitimate entity" whose powers "go far beyond the scope of the Chemical Weapons Convention." This is a jurisdictional challenge, distinct from procedural and substantive contestation.

2.2.3 UNTSO and the bounded contest window

Status: $\mathcal{V}$ (Validated). The UNTSO mechanism after Resolution 2803 requires "predictable access modalities, disciplined evidentiary thresholds, and a dispute-processing routine that allows parties to contest conclusions within a bounded process." The modality "shift[s] the locus of dispute from motives to method through a bounded contest window."

2.2.4 New START expiration

Status: $\mathcal{V}$ (Validated). New START expired on 5 February 2026, after inspections had been halted since 2020 and formally suspended in 2023.

2.3 Political economy of verification

2.3.1 Situation-structure framework

Status: $\mathcal{V}$ (Validated). Zurn's situation-structure framework distinguishes between Prisoners' Dilemma, coordination, and assurance games. "Situation-structuralism distinguishes collaboration games like Prisoners' Dilemma... from coordination games—an interdependence situation with a problem of collectively choosing from several pareto-efficient equilibria." Different commitment domains correspond to different game structures, requiring different regime designs.

2.3.2 Hegemonic stability theory

Status: $\mathcal{V}$ (Validated). "Previous work on this subject in the rational-choice tradition has emphasized the 'theory of hegemonic stability': that is, the view that concentration of power in one dominant state facilitates the development of strong regimes, and that fragmentation of power is associated with regime collapse."

2.3.3 Coordination games and focal points

Status: $\mathcal{V}$ (Validated). "While game theorists proposed formal solutions to the 'coordination problem,' Schelling (1960) suggested that focal points, or prominent features of the strategic landscape, could facilitate tacit agreements between states."

2.3.4 The verification paradox

Status: $\mathcal{V}$ (Validated). The IAEA experience, the OPCW legitimacy crisis, and the New START expiration all confirm that verification is most needed where political will is weakest, but political will is a precondition for verification.

2.4 What the literature does not resolve

· No controlled comparison exists between declaratory, independent, distributed, and two-tier verification regimes under identical crisis conditions.
· No formal model distinguishes validity, reliability, and legitimacy as separate measurable quantities.
· No experiment has estimated the effect of endogenous error correlation $\rho$ on the marginal value of adding verification sources.
· No protocol specifies the conditions under which contestation improves rather than degrades performance.
· No study has pre-registered a minimum informative effect size for verification regimes.
· No model exists for regime formation and equilibrium selection in the context of distributed verification.
· No work formalizes the cost of maintaining an independence-audit infrastructure.
· No work models discrete infrastructure-capture events as a distinct correlation mechanism.
· No work integrates the situation-structure of the commitment domain into the verification regime design.
· No work models the strategic interaction between adversarial edge-targeting and state edge-protection in a provenance graph.
· No work models the depreciation of normative legitimacy under great-power exemption.

2.5 Methodological anchors

· Pre-registration. OSF template; ADEMP-PreReg template (version 1.1, Zenodo).
· ADEMP framework. Aims, Data-generating mechanisms, Estimands, Methods, Performance measures.
· Effective sample size under correlation. Kish formula: $n_{\text{eff}} = M / (1 + (M-1)\rho)$. Recent literature notes that the correlation Kish's formula needs is the correlation of the exceedance indicators, not the score correlation.
· Smallest Effect Size of Interest (SESOI / MIES). Lakens (2017).
· Restricted mean survival time (RMST). Preferred when censoring is heavy.
· Weighted mean survival time (WMST). "Numerical simulations on two-sample tests confirm that the proposed WMST testing method have higher power than RMST in late difference and early crossing survival curves scenarios."
· Competing risks. Fine–Gray subdistribution hazard model.
· Contestability. Hildebrandt's framework.
· Differential privacy. Lightweight local differential privacy on metadata fields.
· Hardware roots of trust. TRIS and IBX II precedents.

