Chapter 8. The Four Regimes

8.1 Regime $R_0$: declaratory

Self-declared; no contestation; low cost; high vulnerability. Appropriate for $\Sigma = \text{CO}$ with low stakes.

8.2 Regime $R_1$: independent verification

Single independent institution; internal review; moderate-to-high cost; vulnerability to institutional capture and single-point failure. Appropriate for $\Sigma = \text{CO}$ with high stakes.

8.3 Regime $R_2$: distributed, contestable

At least two independent sources, of which at least one non-state; contestation bounded by $(\tau_{\max}, \theta_{\text{adm}}, \theta_{\text{proof}}, K, d)$; fail-safe status rule; authentication maturity model; humanitarian deposit multiplier; independence-audit cost term. Appropriate for $\Sigma = \text{AS}$.

8.4 Regime $R_3$: two-tier distributed verification

Tier 1: medium and small powers as verifiable parties. Tier 2: great powers as observers, subject to the reciprocity pathway. Appropriate for $\Sigma = \text{PD}$.

8.5 Regime-situation matching table

Situation structure Appropriate regime Rationale
PD $R_3$ Enforcement requires great-power participation; two-tier is a concession to feasibility.
CO $R_1$ Coordination requires a focal point; a single institution provides it.
AS $R_2$ Assurance requires transparency and reassurance; distributed verification reduces single-point failure.

