Chapter 10. Falsification Criteria

Criterion Rule
Detection efficacy If $\Delta_{21}^{\text{RMST}} < 0.20$, $H_1$ not supported
False positives Non-inferiority margin exceeded vs $R_1$
Cost Administrative cost + independence-audit cost $> 15\%$ of total agreement cost
Participation Drop $> 25\%$ vs $R_0$
Manipulation Vulnerability exceeds pre-registered margin under coordinated attack
Rights Any violation of a mandatory constraint is a design failure
Humanitarian suspension If simulation shows aid suspended during $Q_t = U$, design fails
Graceful degradation Under majority capture, status must transition to $I$
Equilibrium selection If no exogenous catalyst is present, regime must not be claimed self-sustaining
Legitimacy contestation If legitimacy contestation indefinitely blocks findings, design fails
Discrete capture If Poisson capture events cause $\hat{\rho} > \rho_{\max}$ without triggering fail-safe, design fails
Sensor authentication If sources without authentication produce non-inferior detection, the maturity model is not empirically necessary
Situation-structure mismatch If $R_2$ outperforms $R_1$ in a CO domain, or $R_1$ outperforms $R_2$ in an AS domain, Proposition 3.2 is falsified
Capture-game equilibrium If observed protection levels deviate from $q_e^*$ by more than a pre-registered margin, the capture game is misspecified
Normative contagion If $\Lambda$ does not depreciate under great-power exemption, the normative contagion model is falsified
Reciprocity pathway If great-power verification levels do not increase under the reciprocity pathway, the pathway is not credible
WMST vs RMST If WMST does not achieve higher power than RMST when curves cross, Proposition 6.2 is falsified

