Chapter 14. Limitations

1. No empirical validation of the architecture is claimed.
2. Simulation, when eventually executed, will not prove real-world efficacy.
3. Results depend on model assumptions, especially on $\rho$ and the functional form of endogenous correlation.
4. Real states may behave in ways not captured by utility functions.
5. Technical primitives (zkSNARKs, BFT) may be unavailable, too costly, or inappropriate.
6. $\rho$ is unobservable in practice; $\hat{\rho}$ is a proxy.
7. The political economy of verification is now integrated but remains under-quantified.
8. The independence budget, closed information environment, and exit and re-entry protocol lack direct empirical precedent.
9. Equilibrium selection is not guaranteed by any internal mechanism.
10. The two-tier architecture $R_3$ sacrifices universality for feasibility.
11. The verification paradox is not resolved; it is reframed as a research agenda.
12. The MIES of 20% is derived from illustrative parameters, not from empirical estimation.
13. Legitimacy contestation cannot be eliminated by design.
14. The zkSNARK layer proves consistency, not truth.
15. The BFT guarantee holds only for unsigned messages.
16. The discrete-capture Poisson process is a modeling choice, not an empirical estimate.
17. The independence-audit cost term is a modeling construct; no empirical estimate exists.
18. The humanitarian deposit multiplier $\lambda_h$ is a design parameter; its optimal value is unknown.
19. The situation-structure framework is a typology, not a measurement instrument. Misclassification risk is real.
